<section data-state="rec">
    <h2>What if we recorded reality?</h2>
    <div class="card-row">
        <div class="card fragment">
            <h4>1. Record</h4>
            <p>The test makes the real calls <b>once</b> and saves every request / response pair.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card violet fragment">
            <h4>2. Commit</h4>
            <p>The recording lives next to the test and is reviewed in the pull request like code.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card green fragment">
            <h4>3. Replay</h4>
            <p>Every following run replays the responses, <b>without the network</b>.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="fragment callout">
        The idea is not new: <b>php-vcr</b> has been doing it for years.<br>
        Since Symfony 8.2, it is <b>built into HttpClient</b>, with no external dependency.
    </p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>So what if we took the best of both worlds? What if we recorded reality?</p>
        <p>[click] Step one: record. The test makes the real calls only once, and saves every request / response pair to a file.</p>
        <p>[click] Step two: commit. That file lives with the test. It's reviewed in the pull request, like code. If the API changes, you see it in the diff.</p>
        <p>[click] Step three: replay. Every following run replays the recorded responses, without the network. Fast, stable, no secrets at all. And realistic, because these are real responses.</p>
        <p>[click] Let me be honest: the idea isn't new. It's record and replay, made popular by VCR in Ruby, and php-vcr has been doing it in PHP for years. That's where the cassette comes from, by the way.</p>
        <p>What's new is that since Symfony 8.2, it's built into the HttpClient component. No external dependency, no hook on the curl functions, and driven directly from your tests by the PHPUnit Bridge.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
